The ring loop's trace (docs/BENCHMARKS.md, "The ring's tail latency, and
300,000 connections"): counters on runtime/runtime-net.c's nr_pump, nr_collect
and nr_poll_wait, printed to stderr once a second when NR_TRACE is set. It is
not in the runtime: bench/handler-ring/trace.sh applies it for one build and
reverts it. git apply skips this text.

Both lines start with t, the CLOCK_MONOTONIC second the line was printed at,
the clock loadgen's win_lo and win_hi are on: a line whose t lies in
(win_lo + 1, win_hi] covers one second of the measured window.

`trace` line, per second. Counts: cycles (returns of nr_poll_wait), pumps,
ev (completions pumped), waits, sleeps, sleep_full. The longest pass, a pass
running from one return of nr_poll_wait to the next, less its sleep in
epoll_wait: max_cycle, its length, ms; max_out, its time outside
nr_poll_wait, in the handler, ms; n_at_max, the ready slots the previous
return handed the handler, the ones that time served; max_cycle_pumps and
max_cycle_ev, its pumps and the completions they took. The longest pump:
max_pump, ms, and max_pump_ev, its completions. Maxima over every pass of
the second, not of the longest one: max_in (one call of nr_poll_wait), max_wait
(one nish_uring_wait), max_sleep (one epoll_wait), ms; max_rounds (waits in one
pump), max_npend (sends queued at a pump's end). h(>1,2,5,10,20,50,100):
passes longer than each bound, ms.

`tr2` line, per second: ages, the ready-list entries the handler took;
max_age, the longest any waited from listing to collection, ms, an upper
bound: nr_collect re-appends every entry it reports and stamps it then, so an
entry's age can start before the event it is next collected for; ah(...),
entries older than each bound, ms, with the same caveat; max_list, the longest the list was, read
every 64th collection; and the ring set's taskrun, overflow, nobufs and
requeues over the second and its live count (nish_uring_stats).

